<header class="page-header">
  <p class="page-header__eyebrow">Topic</p>
  <h1 class="page-header__title">Mycelium: the invisible network underfoot</h1>
  <p class="page-header__lead">
    The mushroom we pick is only the fruiting body — the organism
    itself lives as a web of hyphae in soil and wood. This section
    gathers routes into that hidden network: mycorrhizal
    partnerships with tree roots, ecological ideas and the analogy
    between living and computational networks.
  </p>
</header>

<section>
  <h2>Start with the fungal web</h2>
  <p>
    Three routes meet here: an introduction to mycorrhiza, the
    ecological vision of "mycelium running" projects, and the
    idea of networks connecting organisms. Choose one or walk
    them in order.
  </p>
  <p>
    The introductory article <a href="{{ '/en/stati/mikoriza/' | url }}">on mycorrhiza</a>
    explains the partnership between fungi and plant roots —
    the dominant life strategy of most forest mushrooms.
    The review of <a href="{{ '/en/stati/mycelium-running/' | url }}">"Mycelium Running"</a>
    widens the picture to ecological applications.
  </p>

  <h2>Look at the network from the other side</h2>
  <p>
    A mycelium is not a metaphor but a working infrastructure:
    it transports nutrients, signals and metabolites between
    distant parts of the forest floor. Mycorrhizal networks link
    trees of different species into shared resource systems —
    which is why a single fruiting body tells you so little about
    the organism that made it.
  </p>

  <h2>From idea to evidence</h2>
  <p>
    In the <a href="{{ '/en/temy/mikoremediatsiya/' | url }}">mycoremediation topic</a>
    you can see how specific mechanisms and measured results are
    discussed. And the <a href="{{ '/en/temy/stamets/' | url }}">guide to Stamets' work</a>
    helps connect the books with the research directions they
    grew from.
  </p>
</section>

